{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","statement":"Primary myelofibrosis is a disease in which abnormal blood cells and fibers build up inside the bone marrow.","source_span":"Primary myelofibrosis is a disease in which abnormal blood cells and fibers build up inside the bone marrow."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","statement":"Primary myelofibrosis is also called chronic idiopathic myelofibrosis.","source_span":"In primary myelofibrosis (also called chronic idiopathic myelofibrosis)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","statement":"In primary myelofibrosis, the web of fibers inside the bone marrow becomes very thick like scar tissue and slows the blood-forming tissue's ability to make blood cells.","source_span":"The web of fibers inside the bone marrow also becomes very thick (like scar tissue) and slows the blood-forming tissues ability to make blood cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","statement":"To make up for the low number of blood cells made in the bone marrow, the liver and spleen begin to make blood cells.","source_span":"In order to make up for the low number of blood cells made in the bone marrow, the liver and spleen begin to make the blood cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","statement":"Symptoms of primary myelofibrosis include pain below the ribs on the left side and feeling very tired.","source_span":"Symptoms of primary myelofibrosis include pain below the ribs on the left side and feeling very tired."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","statement":"Primary myelofibrosis often does not cause early signs or symptoms and may be found during a routine blood test.","source_span":"Primary myelofibrosis often does not cause early signs or symptoms. It may be found during a routine blood test."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","statement":"Prognosis for primary myelofibrosis depends on the age of the patient, the number of abnormal red and white blood cells, the number of blasts in the blood, whether there are certain chromosome changes, and whether the patient has fever, night sweats, or weight loss.","source_span":"The age of the patient.    - The number of abnormal red blood cells and white blood cells.    - The number of blasts in the blood.    - Whether there are certain changes in the chromosomes.    - Whether the patient has signs such as fever, night sweats, or weight loss."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","statement":"Treatment of primary myelofibrosis in patients without signs or symptoms is usually watchful waiting.","source_span":"Treatment of primary myelofibrosis in patients without signs or symptoms is usually watchful waiting."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","statement":"Anemia in primary myelofibrosis is usually treated with transfusion of red blood cells, and may also be treated with erythropoietic growth factors, prednisone, danazol, or thalidomide/lenalidomide/pomalidomide with or without prednisone.","source_span":"Anemia is usually treated with transfusion of red blood cells to relieve symptoms and improve quality of life. In addition, anemia may be treated with: -  Erythropoietic growth factors.     -  Prednisone.     -  Danazol.     -  Thalidomide, lenalidomide, or pomalidomide, with or without prednisone."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","statement":"Treatment of primary myelofibrosis in patients with other signs or symptoms may include targeted therapy with ruxolitinib, chemotherapy, donor stem cell transplant, splenectomy, radiation therapy, or biologic therapy with interferon alfa.","source_span":"Targeted therapy with ruxolitinib.     -  Chemotherapy.     -  Donor stem cell transplant. ... -  Splenectomy.     -  Radiation therapy to the spleen, lymph nodes, or other areas ... -  Biologic therapy using interferon alfa or erythropoietic growth factors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","fact_refs":[0,2],"question":"What happens to the bone marrow in primary myelofibrosis?","answer":"Abnormal blood cells and fibers build up inside the bone marrow, and the web of fibers becomes very thick like scar tissue, slowing the bone marrow's ability to make blood cells."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What is another name for primary myelofibrosis?","answer":"Primary myelofibrosis is also called chronic idiopathic myelofibrosis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are two common symptoms of primary myelofibrosis?","answer":"Common symptoms include pain below the ribs on the left side and feeling very tired."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What five factors affect the prognosis of primary myelofibrosis?","answer":"Prognosis depends on the patient's age, the number of abnormal red and white blood cells, the number of blasts in the blood, whether there are certain chromosome changes, and whether the patient has fever, night sweats, or weight loss."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What is the usual treatment for primary myelofibrosis patients who have no signs or symptoms?","answer":"Treatment is usually watchful waiting."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_2","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How is anemia typically treated in patients with primary myelofibrosis?","answer":"Anemia is usually treated with red blood cell transfusion, and may also be treated with erythropoietic growth factors, prednisone, danazol, or thalidomide, lenalidomide, or pomalidomide with or without prednisone."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","statement":"Essential thrombocythemia is a disease in which too many platelets are made in the bone marrow.","source_span":"Essential thrombocythemia is a disease in which too many platelets are made in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","statement":"Essential thrombocythemia causes an abnormal increase in the number of platelets made in the blood and bone marrow.","source_span":"Essential thrombocythemia causes an abnormal increase in the number of platelets made in the blood and bone marrow."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","statement":"Patients with essential thrombocythemia may have no signs or symptoms, and it is often found during a routine blood test.","source_span":"Patients with essential thrombocythemia may have no signs or symptoms. Essential thrombocythemia often does not cause early signs or symptoms. It may be found during a routine blood test."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","statement":"Symptoms of essential thrombocythemia include headache, burning or tingling in the hands or feet, redness and warmth of the hands or feet, and vision or hearing problems.","source_span":"Headache.    - Burning or tingling in the hands or feet.    - Redness and warmth of the hands or feet.    - Vision or hearing problems."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","statement":"When there are too many platelets, they may clump together and make it hard for blood to flow, and clots may form in blood vessels, causing serious health problems such as stroke or heart attack.","source_span":"When there are too many platelets, they may clump together and make it hard for the blood to flow. Clots may form in blood vessels and there may also be increased bleeding. These can cause serious health problems such as stroke or heart attack."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for essential thrombocythemia depend on the age of the patient and whether the patient has signs or symptoms or other related problems.","source_span":"The age of the patient.    - Whether the patient has signs or symptoms or other problems related to essential thrombocythemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","statement":"Treatment of essential thrombocythemia in patients younger than 60 years with no signs or symptoms and an acceptable platelet count is usually watchful waiting.","source_span":"Treatment of essential thrombocythemia in patients younger than 60 years who have no signs or symptoms and an acceptable platelet count is usually watchful waiting."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","statement":"Treatment options for other essential thrombocythemia patients may include chemotherapy, anagrelide therapy, biologic therapy with interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alpha, and platelet apheresis.","source_span":"Chemotherapy.     -  Anagrelide therapy.     -  Biologic therapy using interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alpha.     - Platelet apheresis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is essential thrombocythemia?","answer":"Essential thrombocythemia is a disease in which too many platelets are made in the bone marrow, causing an abnormal increase in the number of platelets in the blood and bone marrow."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What symptoms may be caused by essential thrombocythemia?","answer":"Symptoms can include headache, burning or tingling in the hands or feet, redness and warmth of the hands or feet, and vision or hearing problems."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What serious health problems can result when too many platelets clump together in essential thrombocythemia?","answer":"Clumping platelets can form clots in blood vessels and cause serious health problems such as stroke or heart attack."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What two factors affect prognosis and treatment options for essential thrombocythemia?","answer":"Prognosis and treatment depend on the age of the patient and whether the patient has signs, symptoms, or other related problems."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is the usual treatment for essential thrombocythemia patients under 60 with no symptoms and an acceptable platelet count?","answer":"Treatment is usually watchful waiting."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_3","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What treatment options exist for essential thrombocythemia patients who need active treatment?","answer":"Options include chemotherapy, anagrelide therapy, biologic therapy with interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alpha, and platelet apheresis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","statement":"Chronic eosinophilic leukemia is a disease in which too many white blood cells (eosinophils) are made in the bone marrow.","source_span":"Chronic eosinophilic leukemia is a disease in which too many white blood cells (eosinophils) are made in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","statement":"Eosinophils are white blood cells that react to allergens and help fight infections caused by certain parasites.","source_span":"Eosinophils are white blood cells that react to allergens (substances that cause an allergic response) and help fight infections caused by certain parasites."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","statement":"In chronic eosinophilic leukemia, there are too many eosinophils in the blood, bone marrow, and other tissues.","source_span":"In chronic eosinophilic leukemia, there are too many eosinophils in the blood, bone marrow, and other tissues."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","statement":"Chronic eosinophilic leukemia may stay the same for many years or may progress quickly to acute leukemia.","source_span":"Chronic eosinophilic leukemia may stay the same for many years or it may progress quickly to acute leukemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","statement":"Signs and symptoms of chronic eosinophilic leukemia include fever and feeling very tired.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of chronic eosinophilic leukemia include fever and feeling very tired."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","statement":"Other symptoms of chronic eosinophilic leukemia include cough, swelling under the skin around the eyes, lips, throat, hands, or feet, muscle pain, itching, and diarrhea.","source_span":"Cough.    - Swelling under the skin around the eyes and lips, in the throat, or on the hands and feet.    - Muscle pain.    - Itching.    -  Diarrhea."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","statement":"Treatment of chronic eosinophilic leukemia may include bone marrow transplant, biologic therapy using interferon alfa, or a clinical trial of a new treatment.","source_span":"Bone marrow transplant.     -  Biologic therapy using interferon alfa.     - A clinical trial of a new treatment."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is chronic eosinophilic leukemia?","answer":"It is a disease in which too many white blood cells called eosinophils are made in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What is the normal function of eosinophils in the body?","answer":"Eosinophils are white blood cells that react to allergens and help fight infections caused by certain parasites."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Can chronic eosinophilic leukemia turn into acute leukemia?","answer":"Yes, chronic eosinophilic leukemia may stay the same for many years or may progress quickly to acute leukemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the two main signs and symptoms of chronic eosinophilic leukemia?","answer":"The two main signs and symptoms are fever and feeling very tired."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Besides fever and fatigue, what other symptoms can occur in chronic eosinophilic leukemia?","answer":"Other symptoms can include cough, swelling under the skin around the eyes, lips, throat, hands, or feet, muscle pain, itching, and diarrhea."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000013_2_5","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What treatment options are available for chronic eosinophilic leukemia?","answer":"Treatment options include bone marrow transplant, biologic therapy using interferon alfa, or a clinical trial of a new treatment."}
